We will just use nine columns in our analysis:

* **Site** - breeding site name used throughout the publication
* **winterLongitude** - longitude of the winter migration site
* **winterLatitude** - latitude of the winter migration site
* **pslon** - longitude of the post-Saharan stopover site
* **pslat** - latitude of the post-Saharan stopover site
* **iblon** - longitude of the Iberian stopover site
* **iblat** - latitude of the Iberian stopover site
* **awestlon** - longitude of the westernmost location
* **awestlat** - latitude of the westernmost location


## The pipe symbol {#sec-pipe .core}

[↑ top](#)

[**Core**]{.core-text}

We are going to make copies of these data frames with only the columns we are interested in.

To do this, we first need to learn about the **pipe** symbol `|>`. This is an **operator** in R which is used to link multiple steps in an analysis.

For example:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
my_numbers <- c(2, 4, 6, 8)

my_numbers |> sum()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 20
```


:::
:::


The pipe passes the value on its left into the function on its right. Here, we first make a **vector** of numbers, then we use this vector as the input to the function `sum()`.

This produces the same result as:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
my_numbers <- c(2, 4, 6, 8)

sum(my_numbers)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 20
```


:::
:::


Pipes make it easier to perform a series of steps on the same variable:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
my_numbers <- c(2, 4, 6, 8)

my_numbers |>
  sum() |>
  sqrt()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 4.472136
```


:::
:::


You can keep adding additional steps to your pipe:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
my_numbers <- c(2, 4, 6, 8)

my_numbers |>
  sum() |>
  sqrt() |>
  round()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 4
```


:::
:::



We can assign the result to a variable using the `<-` operator as usual.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
my_numbers <- c(2, 4, 6, 8)

my_result <- my_numbers |>
  sum() |>
  sqrt() |>
  round()

print(my_result)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 4
```


:::
:::


